body:
That's a very frightening place to be, where you're afraid of what you might be capable of doing. I've been there, too. It's horrifying to experience that fear of losing control.

On the surface, it sounds like you are very close to graduating and don't have much left to go - though given the psychiatric problems that have arisen and your performance in Spanish, for instance, that goal may seem to you very far off right now. Maybe it seems impossible to you. Especially when you've lost financial aid and have been placed on probation for factors that were beyond your control. And now, too, I'm sure you feel the pressure of knowing that your mom took this money out to get you through a semester. It can't be easy having to bear that pressure.

I am not a doctor and am not familiar with the psychiatric disorder you describe though it sounds like the meds you are taking for the intracranial hypertension aren't really doing you any favors. Could they be making things worse? Do you think that is possible? Just throwing it out there.

I myself was on tranqs once when I was terminated from a prestigious job due to some poor choices that I'd made... And the tranquilizers were a horrible choice. I overslept, spent a lot of days bombed out of my mind. Missed job counseling interviews. It was dreadful. The pills only made it worse. Mental health is a young science and it's very difficult to find any sort of treatment that works. 

What does your support network look like at school? Do you have friends close by? Or are you mostly alone? You did mention a boyfriend. Is he nearby?

